-- title:
--   Trace-preserving subspace: $\|\pi(X)\|_{2,B}=\|X\|_{2,A}$ and $\pi$ injective on $V$
-- statement:
--   Let $H_A=\mathbb C^{N_A}$ and $H_B=\mathbb C^{N_B}$ be finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, let $a_1,\dots,a_s$ be operators on $H_A$ and $b_1,\dots,b_s$ operators on $H_B$ (no further assumption on them), and let $\mathcal A=C^*(I_A,a_1,\dots,a_s)$ be the unital $*$-algebra they generate. Let $\pi:\mathcal A\to B(H_B)$ be a unital $*$-homomorphism with $\pi(a_i)=b_i$. Write $\tau_L(X)=\operatorname{Tr}X/N_L$ for the normalized trace, $\|X\|_{2,L}=\sqrt{\tau_L(X^*X)}$, $\mathcal F^A_t$ for the span of the identity and of all marked words $a_{i_1}^{\epsilon_1}\cdots a_{i_\ell}^{\epsilon_\ell}$ with $\ell\le t$ and $\epsilon_j\in\{1,*\}$, and $d_{\mathrm{rel}}\in\mathbb N_0\cup\{+\infty\}$ for the relative distance (Definition 4.1).
--
--   If a complex subspace $V\subseteq\mathcal A$ satisfies
--
--   $$\tau_B(\pi(X)^*\pi(Y))=\tau_A(X^*Y)\qquad(X,Y\in V),$$
--
--   then
--
--   1. $\|\pi(X)\|_{2,B}=\|X\|_{2,A}$ for every $X\in V$, and
--   2. $\pi$ is injective on $V$.
--
--   The degree–distance criterion applies this observation to $V=\mathcal F^A_D$; injectivity is what allows the source label to be uncomputed in the relative-decoding circuit.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: Hilbert spaces H_A, H_B with N_L = dim H_L ≥ 1 (τ_L divides by N_L) → index types nA, nB may be empty, where Lean's x/0 = 0 gives τ_L ≡ 0; reason: covers only extra degenerate instances where the source's τ_L is undefined; on nonempty index types it is the source's statement verbatim
--   - encoding: A = C∗(I_A, a₁,…,a_s) ⊆ B(H_A), π : A ↠ B unital ∗-homomorphism with π(aᵢ) = bᵢ → A = StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a) in Matrix nA nA ℂ; π : A →⋆ₐ[ℂ] Matrix nB nB ℂ with π (gen a i) = b i; reason: in finite dimension the generated ∗-subalgebra is norm closed (App. E, p.85); the range of π is the ∗-subalgebra generated by the bᵢ, i.e. B, so 'onto B' is automatic
--   - encoding: App. A.4 states (357)–(358) for an arbitrary represented algebra 𝒜 ⊆ B(ℋ_A) and a subspace 𝒱 ⊆ 𝒜 → 𝒜 is presented as C∗(I_A, a₁,…,a_s) by a finite family a with b = π ∘ a; reason: every finite-dimensional unital ∗-subalgebra of B(ℋ_A) is generated by a finite basis, and b, hπ only name the images of the generators, so the same instances are covered
-- source:
--   Shang, Stilck França, From Simple Sources to Quantum Advantage: Homomorphic Polynomial Transduction via Relative Decoding, arXiv:2609.35101 (held version, sha256 d2be7859…), Appendix A.4, eqs. (357)–(358), p.68 (pdf_page 68)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LIDT_MIPStarRE_Quantum_FiniteMatrix_NormalizedTrace
import Definitions.Def_RelDecoding_markedSpace
import Definitions.Def_RelDecoding_norm2

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RelDecoding

open LIDT.MIPStarRE.Quantum

/-- **App. A.4, (357) ⇒ (358): isometry and injectivity on a trace-preserving subspace.** If a
subspace `V ⊆ A` satisfies `τ_B(π(X)∗π(Y)) = τ_A(X∗Y)` for all `X, Y ∈ V`, then
`∥π(X)∥_{2,B} = ∥X∥_{2,A}` for every `X ∈ V`, and `π` is injective on `V`. Shang, Stilck França,
*From Simple Sources to Quantum Advantage: Homomorphic Polynomial Transduction via Relative
Decoding*, arXiv:2609.35101 (held version, sha256 d2be7859…), Appendix A.4, eqs. (357)–(358),
p.68.

**Formalization Note.** `A = C∗(I_A, a)` is `StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a)`; `V` is any
complex subspace of `A`; `π` is a unital ∗-homomorphism from `A` into `Matrix nB nB ℂ` with
`π(aᵢ) = bᵢ`; `τ_L = Tr / N_L` and `∥X∥_{2,L} = √(τ_L(X∗X))` (`norm2`). The index types may be
empty (then `τ ≡ 0` by Lean's `x / 0 = 0`). -/
theorem isometry_on_subspace {nA nB : Type*} [Fintype nA] [DecidableEq nA] [Fintype nB]
    [DecidableEq nB] {s : ℕ} (a : Fin s → Matrix nA nA ℂ) (b : Fin s → Matrix nB nB ℂ)
    (π : genAlg a →⋆ₐ[ℂ] Matrix nB nB ℂ) (hπ : ∀ i, π (gen a i) = b i)
    (V : Submodule ℂ (genAlg a))
    (hV : ∀ X ∈ V, ∀ Y ∈ V, normalizedTrace (star (π X) * π Y) =
      normalizedTrace (star (X : Matrix nA nA ℂ) * (Y : Matrix nA nA ℂ))) :
    (∀ X ∈ V, norm2 (π X) = norm2 (X : Matrix nA nA ℂ)) ∧ Set.InjOn π (V : Set (genAlg a)) := by sorry

end RelDecoding
